TI  - THE EFFECT OF EXTERNAL LOADING UPON POWER OUTPUT IN STAIR CLIMBING
T2  - EUROPEAN JOURNAL OF APPLIED PHYSIOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - Previous studies have examined man''s ability to produce external power output during maximal repetitive work cycles of short duration. It appears that there were methodological limitations which could inherently mask man''s true capacities. The effect of variable external loads upon external power output as measured by running upstairs was examined. Fourteen male subjects (16-31 yr old) who regularly participated in competitive sports performed maximal stair step tests under 5 experimental loading conditions (no external load, 10.1, 19.2, 24.2 and 29.2 kg). Significant increases (P < 0.05) in external power output were found. External power output increased from a mean of 15.9 W .cntdot. kg-1 .+-. 1.0 (unloaded condition) to 18.5 W .cntdot. kg-1 .+-. 1.5 (external load of 29.2 kg). This is the 1st modern investigation demonstrating that external loads effect external power output, as measured by this technique.
