AB  - Purpose This study compared selected physiological and autonomic responses induced by swimming training sets. Methods Thirty male swimmers (19 +/- 3 years) performed maximal efforts of 50- (s50) and 400-m (maximal aerobic speed; MAS) and an incremental test to determine the speed corresponding to lactate threshold (sLT). In part 1, 28 swimmers performed 8 x 200-m (intensity - passive recovery: sLT - 30 s) and 8 x 100-m (MAS - 30 s). In part 2, three groups of ten swimmers performed 8 x 50-m (90% s50-30 s), 4 x 50-m (maximal - 2 min), 8 x 25-m (maximal - 2 min). Blood lactate (BL) was determined at start, middle, and end; area under the curve (BLarea) and rate of increase (BLrate) were calculated. Blood pH and heart rate variability (HRV) were determined pre- and post-sets. Internal load was quantified using session rating of perceived exertion and training impulse. Results BLrate was higher in 8 x 100-m vs. 8 x 200-m (0.37 +/- 0.20, 0.18 +/- 0.12 mmol & sdot;L- 1 & sdot;min(- 1), p < 0.05) and in 8 x 50-m and 4 x 50-m compared to 8 x 25-m (1.42 +/- 0.26, 1.54 +/- 0.39, 0.57 +/- 0.15 mmol & sdot;L- 1 & sdot;min(- 1) respectively, p < 0.05). BLarea was higher in 8 x 200-m compared to 8 x 100-m (63.0 +/- 36.1, 38.2 +/- 20.0 mmol<middle dot>L- 1 & sdot;min) and in 8 x 25-m compared to 8 x 50-m and 4 x 50-m (80.1 +/- 20.9, 49.7 +/- 14.0, 57.4 +/- 17.3 mmol<middle dot>L- 1 & sdot;min, respectively, p < 0.05). Post-set pH was lower in 4 x 50-m compared to 8 x 25-m (7.16 +/- 0.07, 7.25 +/- 0.06, p < 0.05). HRV was lower post-8 x 50-m compared to 8 x 25-m (1.69 +/- 0.42, 2.41 +/- 0.40 ms, p < 0.05). In part 1, but not part 2, training load methods were correlated (r = 0.52, p = 0.01). Conclusions Despite similar BL, evaluating autonomic responses, BLarea, BLrate and acid-base balance are important to facilitate decisions on swimming training planning combined with internal training load assessment.
