AB  - Junior-state female rugby league athletes display significantly less strength and power compared with their senior counterparts, making in-season physical capacity development imperative, given the rolling competition schedule for these athletes. Twelve junior-state female rugby league athletes (age: 17.5 +/- 0.5 years, height: 1.66 +/- 0.05 m, mass: 72.6 +/- 13.0 kg) completed countermovement jump, isometric midthigh pull, 40-m sprint, 505 change of direction, 300-m shuttle, and 30:15 intermittent fitness test at the beginning of preseason (T1), end of preseason (T2), and end of in-season (T3). External training loads for field-based (GPS metrics) and resistance-based (volume load [VL]) training sessions were also recorded. Multiple linear mixed models were performed to determine whether significant changes occurred across the season, with linear regressions calculated between any change with the relevant external load measure to determine the interactions. Isometric midthigh pull relative peak force, 0-10-m sprint time, and 505 time significantly improved from T1 to T2. While in T2 to T3, these speed assessments demonstrated significant decrements in performance. Conversely, countermovement jump height and 300-m shuttle time was maintained from T1 to T2 and significantly improved from T2 to T3. Increases in countermovement jump height was associated with a higher VL of lower body maximal and dynamic efforts, while increases in isometric midthigh pull relative peak force were associated with higher lower body repeated and plyometric effort VLs. Additionally, faster 505 times were significantly associated with a greater lower body plyometric effort VL. No GPS metric was associated with any change in running-based metrics. This data may assist practitioners in planning training across a junior-state female rugby league season.
