AB  - Advanced footwear technology (AFT) improves running performance by reducing energetic costs at a given speed. Advancements in midsole foam may also allow athletes to run with reduced training load with lower perceived exertion, leg fatigue and muscle soreness which could contribute to better performance. The purpose of this study was to compare leg fatigue, muscle soreness and training load during prescribed periods between daily training shoes with traditional and advanced midsole foam (ADV), and the change in these variables from de-loading and overloading periods between foam conditions. Eighty-seven runners completed individualized de-loading (two weeks) and overloading (10 d) training periods in shoes with traditional EVA foam (TRAD) and with ADV. Runners uploaded daily training data and completed an online survey on their perceived exertion, leg fatigue and muscle soreness. Non-parametric tests were used to compare variables between shoes. During the overloading period, calf muscle soreness (similar to 19%; p = 0.003; ES = 0.32) and total muscle soreness and leg fatigue (similar to 8%; p = 0.028; ES = 0.24) scores were moderately lower in ADV compared to TRAD shoes. The change in total muscle soreness and leg fatigue score from the de-loading to the overloading periods was slightly greater in TRAD (similar to 116% increase) compared to ADV (similar to 84% increase; p = 0.039; ES = 0.22). Findings suggest ADV may 1) moderately reduce self-reported calf soreness and total soreness and leg fatigue during intense training and, 2) limit the increase in soreness and leg fatigue when training load is increased compared to traditional foam.
