AB  - Wong, DP, Carling, C, Chaouachi, A, Dellal, A, Castagna, C, Chamari, K, and Behm, DG. Estimation of oxygen uptake from heart rate and RPE in young soccer players. J Strength Cond Res 25(7): 1983-1988, 2011-The objective of this study was to estimate the oxygen uptake ((V) over dotO(2)) in elite youth soccer players using measures of heart rate (HR) and ratings of perceived exertion (RPEs). Forty-six regional-level male youth soccer players (similar to 13 years) participated in 2 (V) over dotO(2)max tests. Data for HR, RPE, and (V) over dotO(2) were simultaneously recorded during the (V) over dotO(2)max tests with incremental running speed. Regression equations were derived from the first (V) over dotO(2)max test. Two weeks later, all players performed the same (V) over dotO(2)max test to validate the developed regression equations. There were no significant differences between the estimated values in the first test and actual values in the second test. During the continuous endurance exercise, the combination of percentage of maximal HR (%HRmax) and RPE measures gave similar estimation of %(V) over dotO(2)max (R(2) = 83%) in comparison to %HRmax alone (R(2) = 81%). However, the estimation of (V) over dotO(2) using combined %HRmax and RPE was not satisfactory (R(2) = 45-46%). Therefore, the use of %HRmax (without RPE) to estimate %(V) over dotO(2)max could be a useful tool in young soccer players during field-based continuous endurance testing and training. Specifically, coaches can use the %HRmax to quantify internal loads (%(V) over dotO(2)max) and subsequently implement continuous endurance training at appropriate intensities. Furthermore, it seems that RPE is more useful as a measure of internal load during noncontinuous (e. g., intermittent and sprint) exercises but not to estimate %(V) over dotO(2)max during continuous aerobic exercise (R(2) = 59%).
