TI  - METABOLIC COST OF EXERCISE AND PHYSICAL PERFORMANCE IN CHILDREN WITH SOME OBSERVATIONS ON EXTERNAL LOADING
T2  - EUROPEAN JOURNAL OF APPLIED PHYSIOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - The metabolic cost (.ovrhdot.VO2) of running was studied on a motor-driven treadmill in 9 athletic boys, 5 athletic girls and 9 active boys aged 11-13 yr; the results were compared with their performance times during racing out of doors. In 15 children, the effects of external loading on aerobic power output were observed. .ovrhdot.VO2 was proportional to body weight in children but when expressed in ml/kg per min .ovrhdot.VO2 for a given speed of running was significantly higher in children than expected from previously collected data on adults. There were no significant differences between aerobic cost of running of the athletic boys, girls or the active boys. The increased .ovrhdot.VO2 ml/kg per min in children appeared to be independent of stride length and frequency but external loading equivalent to 5% of body weight reduced .ovrhdot.VO2 (ml/kg per min) particularly at the higher speeds. In young active and athletic children due to their relatively light body weights and highly developed aerobic power outputs, the required frequency of leg movement apparently was not optimally matched to the force necessary to produce the most economic conversion of aerobic energy into mechanical work. In competitive events their performance times were related to their maximal aerobic power output (r = -0.75) but their times were always inferior to those expected from previous aerobic power weight data collected on adult male and female athletes.
